"<strong>Setups in the demo:</strong> Components; Adding; Subtracting; Dot and cross products; River crossing; Force table. This handout uses <strong>Force table</strong>, which the link above opens; the first four setups go with the Vectors worksheet.",
"The picture is the force table from above (<strong>Top view</strong>) or in <strong>3D</strong>; it opens in 3D where the browser can draw it, and there you drag on empty space to turn the view, scroll or pinch to zoom and double click to put the view back. In either view, drag a pulley around the rim to change its string’s angle. Keys on the picture: {#} to {#} picks a string, the left and right arrows turn its pulley by {#}° (with Shift by {#}°), the up and down arrows change its mass by {#} g (with Shift by {#} g), and P pulls or replaces the center pin.",
"<strong>Strings on the table</strong> chooses {#}, {#} or {#} strings. Each string has <strong>Mass on string n, with its hanger</strong> ({#} to {#} kg in {#} g steps) and <strong>Angle of string n</strong> ({#}° to {#}° in {#}° steps); the buttons on the picture add or take off {#} g or {#} g from the chosen string. <strong>Pulley friction, up to the weight of</strong> runs from {#} to {#} g in steps of {#} g and opens at {#} g.",
"<strong>Show the forces as arrows</strong> draws each string’s pull on the ring; <strong>Show the sum tip to tail</strong> draws the forces head to tail. <strong>Pull the pin</strong> takes the center pin out to see where the ring comes to rest without it. <strong>Set the last string to the equilibrant</strong> sets the last string by calculation, rounded to the nearest gram and degree, so it gives the answer away; it is disabled in Blind.",
"<strong>Record this setup</strong> stores every string’s mass and angle, whether the ring is centered and the pulley friction. <strong>Export to worksheet</strong> sends the recorded setups to this handout’s Data table in order: trial, \\(m_1\\) to \\(m_4\\) in kg, \\(\\theta_1\\) to \\(\\theta_4\\) in degrees and whether the ring was centered. <strong>Clear</strong> starts a new series. The CSV copy also records \\(g\\) and the friction setting.",
"Blind hides the table of forces and components, the net force and the equilibrant; the ring still shows whether it is centered, and with the pin out, whether it comes to rest off center, without saying which way.",
"Each string pulls the ring with its tension, which for a hanging mass at rest is its weight: \\(F_i = m_i g\\) with \\(g = 9.81\\ \\mathrm{m/s^2}\\), components \\(F_{ix} = F_i\\cos\\theta_i\\) and \\(F_{iy} = F_i\\sin\\theta_i\\), angles counterclockwise from the {#}° mark. The equilibrant of the other strings is \\(\\vec E = -\\sum \\vec F_i\\), reported as a force, an angle and the mass \\(E/g\\).",
"Pulley friction lets each string’s tension differ from its hanging weight by up to the amount set (given as the weight of that many grams; a chosen value, not a measured one), so the ring centers over a small range of masses and angles, as on a real table. At {#} the pulleys are ideal and the ring centers only when the weights cancel exactly.",
"Model: massless strings, a light ring that slides freely on the table, and every string taken to point at the pin; the ring’s few millimeters of play only move the picture and set where the ring rests when the pin is pulled. The table’s sizes are chosen: strings leave the table {#} cm from the center, the ring is {#} cm across (to the middle of its {#} mm wire) and the pin is {#} mm across, so an unbalanced ring rests against the pin with its center {#} mm toward the net pull.",
"With the pin pulled, the ring is pulled toward the pulleys by the hanging weights and can rest only where those pulls add to zero, the weighted geometric median of the pulleys (where the hangers hang lowest). It is shown sliding slowly along the net pull and stopping at the first place the pulley friction can hold it; when one string outweighs the rest it runs toward the rim and is stopped there, as a real ring would be when a hanger reaches the floor.",
"The 3D view is drawn to the same scale on a table {#} cm across; each hanger drops by as much as the ring moves toward its pulley. A hanger is drawn as {#} g with brass slotted masses (density {#} kg/m³) making up the rest; the split is a picture, only the total matters. The table is simulated: whether the ring centers comes from the model above, so an unchanged setup gives the same result every time.",
"Predict the force that balances two or three given forces, by a scale drawing and by components, and test the prediction on a force table.",
"A light ring sits around a pin at the center of a round table. Each string runs from the ring over a pulley at the rim to a hanging mass. A hanging mass at rest pulls its string with a tension equal to its weight, so string \\(i\\) pulls the ring with a force of size \\(F_i = m_i g\\) along its angle \\(\\theta_i\\), measured counterclockwise from the {#}° mark. Its components are",
"\\[F_{ix} = F_i\\cos\\theta_i,\\qquad F_{iy} = F_i\\sin\\theta_i.\\]",
"The ring stays centered when the forces add to zero, \\(\\sum F_x = 0\\) and \\(\\sum F_y = 0\\). The <strong>equilibrant</strong> of a set of forces is the single force that balances them: equal in size and opposite in direction to their resultant \\(\\vec R\\),",
"\\[\\vec E = -\\vec R,\\qquad R = \\sqrt{R_x^2 + R_y^2},\\qquad \\theta_E = \\theta_R + 180^\\circ.\\]",
